WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:05.170
Since he first appeared on the big screen
in 1962, James Bond has become a worldwide

00:00:05.170 --> 00:00:06.200
film sensation.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:07.220
"Bond.

00:00:09.320 --> 00:00:10.060
James Bond."

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.360
With 24 "official" James Bond adventures and
three "unofficial" films, the franchise can

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:18.380
be a bit overwhelming, especially if you're
new to the series.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:22.120
Some of these movies remain classics today,
while others…not so much.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:26.620
Let's take a look at every James Bond film
ever made, and rank them from worst to best.

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:30.000
Chances are good this list will leave you
shaken and stirred...

00:00:30.200 --> 00:00:32.400
Casino Royale (1967)

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:36.750
Due to some behind-the-scenes drama, producer
Charles Feldman opted to make his version

00:00:36.750 --> 00:00:42.050
of Casino Royale a satire, casting comedic
actor Peter Sellers as one of many James Bonds

00:00:42.050 --> 00:00:45.060
— it gets complicated — and Orson Welles
as Le Chiffre.

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:51.600
Despite being a spoof, 1967's Casino Royale
is more cheesy and cringe-inducing than actually

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:54.600
funny, and can easily be skipped on your next
Bond-a-thon.

00:00:55.320 --> 00:00:58.140
"This is an historic day in our saga Sir James.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:03.340
The day SMUSH finally eliminated the original
James Bond."

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.140
A View to a Kill (1985)

00:01:06.540 --> 00:01:10.920
Roger Moore's final appearance as James Bond
in A View to a Kill is by far his weakest.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:15.259
Moore delivers a performance that could be
described as zombie-like, dozing his way through

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:16.460
nearly every scene.

00:01:16.580 --> 00:01:19.280
Making matters worse, the plot is nearly non-existent.

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:24.080
It centers on a Silicon Valley magnate played
by Christopher Walken who wants to control

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:25.640
the market for computer chips.

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.100
And although Walken and his henchwoman, played
by Grace Jones, do a fine job with what they

00:01:30.100 --> 00:01:33.260
were given, their performances couldn't redeem
this movie.

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:37.060
"So, does anybody else wanna drop out?"

00:01:37.400 --> 00:01:40.040
The Man with the Golden Gun (1974)

00:01:40.360 --> 00:01:44.700
Roger Moore's second outing as Bond in The
Man with the Golden Gun was also pretty shabby.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:49.400
The movie tries too hard to be flashy, and
nearly everything overshadows Moore himself.

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:53.639
The kung-fu scenes seem cheesy and tacked
on, and there's a decided lack of typical

00:01:53.639 --> 00:01:55.130
Bond spy gadgetry — replaced with impossibilities
like flying cars.

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:59.799
On the plus side, the movie does offer two
of the coolest "bad guys" of the series: Christopher

00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:03.460
Lee as Scaramanga and Hervé Villechaize as
his henchman, Nick Nack.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:05.820
Octopussy (1983)

00:02:06.140 --> 00:02:09.720
Director John Glen resorted to a tired formula
for Octopussy.

00:02:09.960 --> 00:02:13.540
Of all the Roger Moore Bond films, this one
might just be the cheesiest.

00:02:13.860 --> 00:02:19.060
Featuring jewelry heists, a cult, and a world
domination storyline involving nuclear weapons,

00:02:19.160 --> 00:02:24.020
Octopussy suffers from an overabundance of
plot and an underwhelming lack of substance.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:26.640
And remember Bond's ridiculous disguises in
the movie?

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:27.900
How could you forget.

00:02:28.260 --> 00:02:30.280
Casino Royale (1954)

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:34.880
James Bond might be a movie icon, but his
first on-screen appearance was actually on

00:02:34.880 --> 00:02:35.380
television.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:41.020
In 1954, Casino Royale was produced as an
episode of the CBS anthology drama called

00:02:41.020 --> 00:02:41.600
Climax!

00:02:41.820 --> 00:02:44.220
Which was actually performed and broadcast
live.

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:49.760
Starring Barry Nelson as Bond and Peter Lorre
as Le Chiffre, the episode was mostly faithful

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:54.700
to the novel, except that Bond is an American
CIA agent instead of MI6.

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:58.820
It's not a terrible take on the stories, per
se, but it doesn't have the production value

00:02:58.820 --> 00:02:59.600
of its peers.

00:02:59.830 --> 00:03:03.540
That said, it's still pretty impressive that
they pulled it off in front of a live audience

00:03:03.540 --> 00:03:04.040
at all.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:06.760
Tomorrow Never Dies (1997)

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:11.740
Pierce Brosnan's second outing as James Bond
was the same suave and smooth double-oh agent

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:16.160
we'd come to know in GoldenEye, and his onscreen
chemistry with Teri Hatcher steams up every

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:16.980
scene they share.

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:21.760
Also, Jonathan Pryce presents a much more
realistic villain than normal: a media mogul

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:24.910
who attempts to start a war in order to boost
his news market share.

00:03:24.910 --> 00:03:27.420
"I may have some breaking news for you, Elliott."

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:35.180
However, despite the shiny exterior and new
arsenal of gadgets, there's very little new

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:39.660
ground explored in Tomorrow Never Dies, and
it plays a little too safe to stand out in

00:03:39.660 --> 00:03:40.260
the series.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:42.860
The World Is Not Enough (1999)

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:47.080
Brosnan's Bond outings might've all been good
in terms of his personality, but there is

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:50.240
still way too much formula in play with The
World is Not Enough.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:54.820
And while most of the supporting cast was
solid — including Sohie Marceau as Elektra

00:03:54.820 --> 00:03:59.790
King and Robert Carlyle as Renard — the
overall acting of the pic took a major hit

00:03:59.790 --> 00:04:03.620
with Denise Richards' portrayal of nuclear
physicist Christmas Jones.

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:04.980
"JAMES."

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:07.740
Die Another Day (2002)

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:12.200
For his final stint as James Bond, Pierce
Brosnan gave his best performance in the role

00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:13.880
since his debut in GoldenEye.

00:04:14.100 --> 00:04:15.600
"You've been busy, have we Mr. Bond?"

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:18.160
"Just surviving Mr. Chang.

00:04:19.380 --> 00:04:20.340
Just surviving."

00:04:20.780 --> 00:04:24.600
Perhaps he had the solid plot to thank — well,
apart from that space laser bit.

00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:29.070
The film successfully evoked the feel of classic
Bond films with throwback imagery like Halle

00:04:29.070 --> 00:04:31.080
Berry's Jinx emerging from the ocean.

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:35.650
And many of the action sequences were actually
exciting, like the fight between Jinx and

00:04:35.650 --> 00:04:37.680
Miranda Frost at the end of the film.

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:41.700
Unfortunately, Die Another Day's special effects
do not always hold up.

00:04:42.020 --> 00:04:44.520
Live and Let Die (1973)

00:04:44.700 --> 00:04:48.520
This movie's main flaws revolve around the
unimpressive main villain Mr. Big and his

00:04:48.530 --> 00:04:52.330
plot to give away free heroin in order to
get a corner on the market.

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:57.390
Despite this, it's still an adequate entry
in the franchise, and Jane Seymour as Solitaire

00:04:57.390 --> 00:04:59.520
remains one of the best Bond girls to date.

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:01.100
Quantum of Solace (2008)

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:06.280
Daniel Craig's second outing as James Bond
suffers some fundamental flaws, like the villain

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:09.930
Dominic Greene and his plot to monopolize
Bolivia's fresh water supplies.

00:05:09.930 --> 00:05:13.000
That's a pretty boring idea for an international
caper.

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:18.260
On the other hand, it's also a pretty dark
film, with Bond set on revenge and not a tongue-in-cheek

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:19.200
quip in sight.

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:24.400
Olga Kurylenko is great as a Bond girl, and
the inferno fight in the final act is the

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:25.520
stuff of nightmares.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:27.240
Spectre (2015)

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:32.580
In this installment, we finally get to see
our hero come face to face with Blofeld, the

00:05:32.580 --> 00:05:34.620
leader of the shadowy organization SPECTRE.

00:05:34.960 --> 00:05:39.600
That might've been exciting in an of itself,
but at times, the film's plot seems a little

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:40.100
contrived.

00:05:40.300 --> 00:05:45.720
That said, the action and stunt sequences
rose to new heights without relying on computer-generated

00:05:45.730 --> 00:05:48.850
imagery, and Monica Bellucci is delightful
as a Bond Girl.

00:05:48.850 --> 00:05:50.740
So, it's still definitely worth your time.

00:05:51.320 --> 00:05:53.240
Moonraker (1979)

00:05:53.600 --> 00:05:57.840
This installment of the Bond franchise features
one of the most unrealistic plots of all.

00:05:57.850 --> 00:06:02.440
A spacecraft is hijacked in order to release
a deadly chemical around the world, and Roger

00:06:02.440 --> 00:06:05.680
Moore's portrayal slips from merely flippant
to positively silly.

00:06:05.840 --> 00:06:09.680
There's still a lot to like about Moonraker
because the campiness is cheerful, and the

00:06:09.780 --> 00:06:11.370
cast does sell the silliness.

00:06:11.370 --> 00:06:15.730
Fans also applauded the return of Jaws, and
the romantic epilogue will still go down as

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:19.140
containing one of the best Bond double-entendres
in history.

00:06:19.500 --> 00:06:20.500
"007."

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:22.760
"My God what's Bond doing?"

00:06:23.320 --> 00:06:25.580
"I think he's attempting re-entry sir."

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:28.240
Diamonds Are Forever (1971)

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:32.900
Sean Connery's return to the Bond fold in
Diamonds Are Forever didn't sparkle quite

00:06:32.919 --> 00:06:34.540
as much as its predecessors.

00:06:34.540 --> 00:06:39.290
The '70s-era Vegas scenery seems a little
too garish for Bond, and the movie ignores

00:06:39.290 --> 00:06:40.660
much of the source material.

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:45.060
However, there are a few great chase scenes
and the badass bodyguards Bambi and Thumper,

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:47.100
so there's a lot to like about the pic, too.

00:06:47.460 --> 00:06:49.400
For Your Eyes Only (1981)

00:06:49.640 --> 00:06:53.840
Director John Glen delivered a delightfully
stripped-down Bond film with For Your Eyes

00:06:53.840 --> 00:06:54.340
Only.

00:06:54.480 --> 00:06:58.840
It approaches the feel of the earliest Bond
movies, and Moore gives his most serious and

00:06:58.840 --> 00:07:00.120
rugged performance yet.

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:04.800
The return to a Cold War threat was a smart
move, as was the decision to nix any large

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:06.880
and overly bombastic set pieces.

00:07:07.120 --> 00:07:11.420
What remains is an action-packed and smartly
shot movie that does have its flaws, but is

00:07:11.420 --> 00:07:13.980
definitely an enjoyable Bond film nonetheless.

00:07:14.420 --> 00:07:16.440
The Living Daylights (1987)

00:07:16.700 --> 00:07:20.700
When Timothy Dalton stepped into the role
after Moore, critics seemed to miss his predecessor's

00:07:20.700 --> 00:07:23.320
campiness and complained about him being too
uptight.

00:07:23.580 --> 00:07:28.120
Part of what helps keep James Bond films entertaining
is the sardonic tongue-in-cheek wit displayed

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:29.080
by Bond himself.

00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:33.500
Nevertheless, Dalton is backed up by good
supporting performances, and the plot isn't

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:38.540
as outlandish as most, centering on the KGB
and a tale of defection and smuggling.

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:43.120
Add in a few good action scenes, and The Living
Daylights redeems itself as a better-than-average

00:07:43.120 --> 00:07:43.620
Bond film.

00:07:43.920 --> 00:07:46.360
Never Say Never Again (1983)

00:07:46.700 --> 00:07:51.740
Even though the story of Never Say Never Again
is mostly rehashed from Thunderball, there's

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:54.800
something about this movie that's more entertaining
than the original.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:59.699
Max von Sydow delivers a wonderful performance
as Blofeld, and Rowan Atkinson is nervously

00:07:59.700 --> 00:08:02.540
hilarious as Bond's handler at the British
Consulate.

00:08:02.660 --> 00:08:06.440
It's comforting to see a more experienced
Sean Connery back in the role he made famous,

00:08:06.440 --> 00:08:10.460
and he's clearly more comfortable and having
more fun in his final farewell to the Bond

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:10.960
franchise.

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:14.760
"Never... Never say never again."

00:08:15.260 --> 00:08:17.800
The Spy Who Loved Me (1977)

00:08:18.040 --> 00:08:22.080
Even though it's not a great Bond film, Roger
Moore started to hit his stride as the lead

00:08:22.080 --> 00:08:24.820
character in his third attempt, The Spy Who
Loved Me.

00:08:25.060 --> 00:08:30.540
His onscreen chemistry with Barbara Bach's
Anya is undeniable, and the film opens with

00:08:30.540 --> 00:08:31.920
a great stunt sequence.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:36.760
While the main villain and his dastardly plans
for world domination are rather lackluster,

00:08:36.770 --> 00:08:42.080
The Spy Who Loved Me does introduce audiences
to one of the all-time greatest Bond henchmen:

00:08:42.080 --> 00:08:42.980
Richard Kiel's Jaws.

00:08:43.200 --> 00:08:47.860
All in all, it's a good entry in the franchise,
and perhaps Moore's best outing as James Bond.

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:50.320
Licence to Kill (1989)

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:55.720
In Timothy Dalton's second — and final — performance
as James Bond, the filmmakers opted to play

00:08:55.720 --> 00:08:59.680
to his strengths, resulting in a much darker
and more violent Bond film.

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:04.000
While there are definitely a few preposterous
moments — like capturing an airplane with

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:08.220
a helicopter — on the whole, Licence to
Kill is also much more sensible than most

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:09.020
Bond movies.

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:13.220
Like all good Bond films, Licence to Kill
has plenty of entertaining action scenes,

00:09:13.220 --> 00:09:16.580
and this time, there's also an entertaining
plot to go along with them.

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:20.380
"Remember you're only President for life."

00:09:21.640 --> 00:09:23.920
You Only Live Twice (1967)

00:09:24.280 --> 00:09:28.200
Even though You Only Live Twice has one of
the most improbable plots of all the Bond

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:32.071
films — involving hijacked spacecraft — its
substantial charms are undeniable.

00:09:32.071 --> 00:09:37.670
The movie boasts some beautiful cinematography,
so, it's easy to forgive the film's wandering

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:42.080
script and preposterous volcano hideout and
count this one as very enjoyable.

00:09:42.560 --> 00:09:45.380
On Her Majesty's Secret Service (1969)

00:09:45.580 --> 00:09:50.340
In his only appearance in the Bond circuit,
George Lazenby is pretty wooden in his delivery.

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:55.240
However, those action sequences paired with
the tone and Diana Rigg's turn as Tracy make

00:09:55.240 --> 00:09:56.120
this one a keeper.

00:09:56.320 --> 00:10:01.420
Not to mention, the villain Blofeld is played
convincingly by Telly Savalas, and the emotional

00:10:01.420 --> 00:10:05.740
punch of the final scene adds a thoroughly
enjoyable installment to the Bond franchise.

00:10:06.140 --> 00:10:07.780
GoldenEye (1995)

00:10:11.020 --> 00:10:15.100
To reinvent the franchise, Pierce Brosnan
took over the role of Bond and managed to

00:10:15.100 --> 00:10:16.440
strike all the right notes.

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:22.100
GoldenEye is a completely original story that
introduces Sean Bean's former MI6 agent Trevelyan

00:10:22.100 --> 00:10:23.380
as the primary villain.

00:10:23.660 --> 00:10:27.900
From the entertaining action sequences to
the extraordinary set pieces, it's no wonder

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:29.940
this movie made for such a rad video game.

00:10:35.120 --> 00:10:37.000
Thunderball (1965)

00:10:37.520 --> 00:10:41.620
As the fourth film in the series, Thunderball
was perfectly placed to capitalize on the

00:10:41.630 --> 00:10:46.660
height of the Cold War, with a plot focusing
on two stolen atomic bombs and Bond's attempt

00:10:46.660 --> 00:10:48.040
to recover them from SPECTRE.

00:10:48.300 --> 00:10:52.260
While it isn't quite as good as its three
predecessors, Thunderball is still a very

00:10:52.270 --> 00:10:56.490
solid Bond movie, with spectacular scenery
and action sequences.

00:10:56.490 --> 00:11:00.200
And while it could've used some judicious
editing, it's still a visual treat.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:02.280
Skyfall (2012)

00:11:02.800 --> 00:11:08.380
For Skyfall, the Bond series took a leap away
from 007 to focus instead on the past of Bond's

00:11:08.380 --> 00:11:14.160
superior, M, and a former MI6 agent turned
terrorist Raoul Silva, played by Javier Bardem.

00:11:14.360 --> 00:11:18.660
Skyfall hits all the right notes for a classic
Bond film, from the exotic locales to the

00:11:18.660 --> 00:11:20.300
compelling plot full of twists.

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:24.700
And let's not forget how Adele's sexy theme
song left people swooning for months after

00:11:24.700 --> 00:11:25.540
this movie dropped.

00:11:26.120 --> 00:11:28.160
Dr. No (1962)

00:11:28.620 --> 00:11:30.880
The very first James Bond film remains a classic.

00:11:31.040 --> 00:11:35.920
All the elements of later Bond films were
introduced here: the stylistic opening credits,

00:11:35.920 --> 00:11:40.660
exotic locales, the James Bond theme, and
his trademark catchphrase, to name a few.

00:11:40.660 --> 00:11:44.120
It's an action-packed 109 minutes of pure
audience escapism.

00:11:44.300 --> 00:11:48.460
Sean Connery is suave and a natural in the
role of Bond, showing why he'd hold on to

00:11:48.460 --> 00:11:50.120
the part for seven different films.

00:11:50.260 --> 00:11:54.340
Only the lack of an impressive henchman for
Dr. No and some pacing issues prevent this

00:11:54.340 --> 00:11:55.700
movie from topping our list.

00:11:56.000 --> 00:12:01.060
The James Bond formula was solidly established
in Dr. No, and was only improved upon in the

00:12:01.080 --> 00:12:02.220
next two installments.

00:12:02.680 --> 00:12:04.880
Casino Royale (2006)

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:10.060
Daniel Craig's first turn as 007 was meant
to introduce a complete reboot of the James

00:12:10.060 --> 00:12:13.950
Bond franchise — set at the very start of
his secret agent career — and it was so

00:12:13.950 --> 00:12:16.520
successful that it breathed new life into
the series.

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:20.920
Instead of treating Bond as window dressing
to go along with jaw-dropping action scenes,

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:25.610
the production team made the wise choice to
actually let Bond's haunted and human personality

00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:26.380
shine through.

00:12:26.660 --> 00:12:31.580
In addition to Craig's convincing performance
and Eva Green's complicated turn as Vesper,

00:12:31.580 --> 00:12:34.420
Mads Mikkelsen plays a convincing and creepy
Le Chiffre.

00:12:34.720 --> 00:12:39.300
Between the acting, the intense action sequences,
and the emotional resonance of the movie,

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:42.660
the new Casino Royale is one of the best in
the James Bond series.

00:12:43.140 --> 00:12:45.540
From Russia with Love (1963)

00:12:45.860 --> 00:12:50.260
Sean Connery's second outing as Bond is even
better than the first, and From Russia with

00:12:50.261 --> 00:12:54.600
Love is also bolstered by standout action
sequences, like the fight scene aboard the

00:12:54.600 --> 00:12:58.740
Orient Express which would inspire numerous
other train scenes in later Bond films.

00:12:58.940 --> 00:13:02.920
Everything is more colorful and there are
several memorable one-liners and funny visual

00:13:02.920 --> 00:13:06.640
gags that make From Russia With Love an example
of Bond at his finest.

00:13:07.200 --> 00:13:09.180
Goldfinger (1964)

00:13:09.460 --> 00:13:14.120
Although the margin between the top four Bond
films is nearly razor-thin, Goldfinger comes

00:13:14.120 --> 00:13:14.760
out on top.

00:13:15.120 --> 00:13:19.400
From the dramatic and "shocking" opening sequence
to amazing gadgetry and the unforgettable

00:13:19.410 --> 00:13:24.029
image of Shirley Eaton painted gold from head
to toe, Goldfinger is a movie that continues

00:13:24.029 --> 00:13:24.820
to impress.

00:13:25.029 --> 00:13:29.340
You'd be hard-pressed to find a better henchman
than Oddjob, and the name of Bond girl "Pussy

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:31.420
Galore" still inspires a giggle today.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:35.680
The laser table and the climactic fight in
the airplane are among the best Bond scenes

00:13:35.680 --> 00:13:40.311
of all time, and Goldfinger was also the first
Bond movie to include the line "shaken, not

00:13:40.320 --> 00:13:40.820
stirred."

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:45.920
Many other Bond films imitated the formula
perfected in Goldfinger, but few come close.

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:47.340
"Who are you?"

